            <ArticleTitle>Intestinal obstruction by giant Meckel's diverticulum. Case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>491-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Most cases of Meckel's diverticulum (MD) are asymptomatic and discovered by chance. Management of MD is controversial. The authors describe an exceptional case of intestinal obstruction caused by a giant MD in a patient who had previously undergone appendectomy. A review of the contradictory literature on this subject leads to the conclusion that careful consideration of clinical and morphological data (patient's age, ASA score, the surgical procedure to be performed, morphology and position of the MD, any fibrotic bands) is required before deciding whether or not to resect an asymptomatic MD.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
